 I'm realizing the error message is more puzzling than it first appeared. The failure happens at a single 2 MiB page allocation, but with only 6 GiB used out of 24 GiB total, that shouldn't fail unless the VPMM pool itself is fragmented or has hit some internal limit. The \"in pool=6.0 GiB\" metric suggests the pool has already grown to 6 GiB, so the issue might be how the pool manages its internal page allocations rather than raw memory availability.\n\nLooking at the memory logs, I can see the progression of allocations—the code matrix taking 3.2 GiB, then the merkle tree adding another 128 MiB—which helps me understand the memory pressure building up. The question is whether other GPU operations outside the VPMM pool are consuming the remaining memory, preventing the pool from expanding further. The peak memory is 6.5 GiB out of 24 GiB available, with most of it in the pool, so there should theoretically be plenty of headroom—unless the GPU has reserved memory for the CUDA context and other overhead that I can't directly observe. Rather than getting stuck on the memory mystery, let me shift focus to the optimization opportunities. Looking at the performance data, Round 0 at APC 300 stands out as the biggest remaining bottleneck at 569ms.\n\nBut I should also dig into the MLE Rounds more carefully since they're showing inverse scaling too—180ms at APC 300 versus 118ms at APC 0. Looking at the kernel breakdown from the profiling data, I'm seeing several logup_zerocheck_mle kernels taking significant time, with the batch and precompute kernels being the main contributors. Looking at the performance breakdown, the GPU kernels account for about 127ms while the total is 180ms, so there's roughly 53ms of CPU overhead. The `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` runs once per AIR instance, and with 791 instances at APC 300, I'm wondering if batching these launches together could help. My main optimization idea is to batch the per-AIR kernel launches into fewer, larger ones like we did with batch-round0, which could cut the ~428ms GKR input evaluation time by half or more through reduced launch overhead and better GPU utilization—potentially saving 200-300ms overall.\n\nThe second approach would be parallelizing the Phase 2 IDFT processing across the 623 AIR results using rayon, which should be straightforward since it's embarrassingly parallel and could save 30-50ms. The third idea involves caching the interaction rule compilation and weight computation across multiple AIR instances to avoid redundant CPU-side setup work.\n\nThen there's the multi-stream pipeline approach using 2-4 CUDA streams to overlap GPU kernel execution with CPU processing, though the synchronization complexity and potential memory management conflicts make this riskier. Finally, I need to address the VPMM out-of-memory issue in the GKR input evaluation that's stemming from a buffer size problem.\n\nThe VPMM OOM is actually blocking all measurements right now, which makes fixing it critical before I can validate any other optimizations. It should be straightforward to resolve by capping the buffer size or using chunked processing. But I'm puzzled—the stacked reduction agent did get measurements, and the batch-round0 agent confirmed the OOM was pre-existing. How did the stacked reduction agent manage to measure if this is blocking everything? Maybe it ran from an earlier code state before the batch-round0 changes, or the OOM only appears under certain GPU conditions. I should try running the benchmark again with a clean GPU state to see if that clears things up.\n\nActually, I'm wondering if the real bottleneck is earlier in the pipeline—the prover might be consuming too much GPU memory during trace generation and commitment before it even gets to constraint evaluation. Looking at the APC 300 output, the stacked matrix commit is allocating substantial memory, especially in the recursion phase. But that's just the second segment; the first segment's trace commit wit